ek on brian ross  investigates on the law and crime trial  network  you can catch brian ross investigates  tuesdays and thursdays at 5 30 p.m  eastern time here on lawn crime network  coming up on law and crime daily nfl  commissioner roger goodell speaks out on  diversity in the wake of a  discrimination lawsuit against the  league plus opening statements will soon  be presented in the case of curtis  reeves still ahead what's expected in  the case that's taken years to go to  trial  [Music]  welcome back after taking a day off  wednesday a full 12-person jury was  selected on thursday in a florida  courtroom for the trial of accused  serial killer robert hayes hayes is  accused of killing three women and  dumping their bodies in the daytona  beach area between november 2005 and  february 2006. authorities allege  laquita gunther julie green and iwana  payton were sex workers haze targeted  hayes later moved to west palm beach  where he's accused in another woman's  2016 murder it was after that alleged  murder when authorities linked hayes to  the three shooting deaths in daytona  beach jury selection consisted of  attorneys asking potential jurors  questions in groups including asking if  they could handle deciding this case as  prosecutors are seeking the death  penalty opening statements are expected  to begin friday  and jury selection stretched into a  fourth day in florida as a former police  captain prepares to face trial eight  years after he allegedly shot and killed  a man in a movie theater former officer  curtis reeves is charged with  second-degree murder in the shooting  death of chad olson rees and olson were  attending a showing of the movie lone  survivor in 2014 when the two got into  an argument over olson texting during  the previews reeves claims olson was  coming at him and said he feared for his  life he says he then shot olson in  self-defense  back in 2017 reeves argued his case in a  stand your ground hearing but a judge  ruled against him on thursday attorneys  began with a pool of 52 potential jurors  working to whittle it down to a group of  10 questioning focus on potential jurors  feeling on carrying weapons while a  traditional jury is made up of 12 people  reeves jury will be made up of six  panelists and four alternates 79 year  old reeves faces a possible life  sentence if convicted opening statements  are expected to start on friday  all right so we've got co-host terry  austin back with us to discuss the  murder in the movie theater terry what  do you think the prosecution's strongest  argument is in this case  you know what based on all the reports  i've heard so far and what the attorneys  have been saying during the voir dire i  think the prosecution has a very strong  argument reeves is being charged with  second-degree murder which means that he  had a depraved mind that he had no  regard for human life and frankly the  fact that he shot someone to death  because they threw popcorn at him and  because they were using a cell phone in  my mind means that he was disregarding  life and he was also charged with  aggravated battery and all you have to  do in florida there is to show that he  was actually using a deadly weapon and  we know he was using a deadly weapon so  i think the prosecution has a very good  case here brian i mean i i would imagine  so especially because reeves lost his  stand your ground hearing doesn't that  mean he legally should have retreated  before using deadly force against olson  absolutely that was a big loss for him  under florida law you have the fact that  you have self-defense but if you see  imminent threat of death or if you see  imminent threat of danger to your body  then of course you can use self-defense  but you do have that duty to retreat  unless there's the castle document  meaning that you are in your own home or  if you can show that you can stand your  ground you don't have that duty to  retreat but here he lost that argument  so i would say he would have to show he  really was an imminent threat of death  or bodily harm and i think he should  have retreated here obviously yeah it's  gonna be interesting how that plays out  with his status of being a former  captain does it help or hurt him you're  gonna have to tune into law and crime  network for capital gable coverage to  see how that plays out when we come back  hear from nfl commissioner roger goodell  on allegations of racism and  discrimination in the league why he says  the nfl fell short in promoting  diversity among its employees  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily  we're hearing from the nfl commissioner  roger goodell for the first time since  the league was slammed with a diversity  lawsuit as co-host darry allison  explains that can mean major changes for  the nfl policies  brian in a press conference this week  commissioner roger goodell said the  league will soon reevaluate current  practices meaning there could be updates  on even removal of the so-called rooney  rule this comes after former miami  dolphins head coach brian flores filed a  lawsuit against the nfl and multiple  teams flores who is black alleges he was  only interviewed for a head coaching job  with the new york giants to fulfill the  rooney rule which requires minority  candidates to be interviewed for head  coaching positions court documents show  text messages sent from patriots head  coach bill belichick to flores  congratulating him on the giant's head  coaching job before he was even  interviewed belichick later apologized  saying he meant to text brian dayball a  white man who was ultimately offered the  position  last week commissioner goodall released  a memo saying the league's efforts to  promote diversity when hiring head  coaches have been unacceptable on  wednesday he doubled down on that saying  that racism and discrimination will not  be tolerated and that changes need to be  made  it's really more important to talk about  what coach flores was talking about and  other coaches have talked about with  respect to what really is happening in  the hiring process what's good what's  bad what's not leading us to the results  that we expect to have  we have spent an extraordinary amount of  time with our committees particularly  our diversity equity and inclusion  committee but also with our membership  in general to sort of talk about the  process  uh i think we made a tremendous amount a  lot of progress in a lot of areas  but not at the head coach  and that is something that uh is  something we really have focused on  to try to get the kind of results that  we would expect and we fell short of  that by a long shot  commissioner goodall said the league  will hire outside experts in diversity  equity and inclusion to review the  league's practices brian  thanks terry samantha flores fight seems  similar to what you do as a civil rights  attorney so how would you evaluate  flores claim against the nfl  i think you know men lie women lie  numbers don't if over 55 percent of the  nfl players are black and less than one  percent of the head coaches are black  those are the numbers with civil rights  cases a lot of times it's on the pla  it's the plaintiff's burden to prove  the discrimination and i think in brian  flora's case he has a smoking gun in  that combined with the rooney rule with  which requires a diverse applicant to be  identified and interviewed he has that  text where  the other candidate the white candidate  is already being congratulated before he  even has the opportunity to be  interviewed so that's something in a  civil rights case you rarely get it's a  smoking gun into um the mindset of the  nfl like hey these guys are just trying  to move around the rules to look like  they're adhering to diversity but in  actuality this is what's really  happening so flores has a very strong  case with that text message as a smoking  gun for sure all right and i'm waiting  to see belichick deposed after that text  message that's that's gonna be the  exciting part terry as the former chief  diversity officer of a fortune 500  company uh what changes do you think the  nfl should implement to make progress  listen there's so much they could do and  commissioner goodell should not have  waited until a class action has been  filed he needed to have implemented  changes long ago you could see that the  rooney rule was not working so you have  to do more and you have to make cultural  changes it starts at the top put your  money where your mouth is you have to  interview candidates look for them get  candidates who are qualified from  diverse backgrounds they're failing to  do that right now they're falling back  on old habits and not really pushing  forward so so much to do yeah we'll see  as this lawsuit progresses thank you  both and thank you for joining us here  on law and crime daily we'll see you  next time as we discuss justice in  america  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

